    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_131'), 'From the architecture proposal, leaderboard section:

  On challenge pass the grader worker runs   ZINCRBY lb:all-time <points> <userId>
  No other table stores points.
  redis.conf:  appendonly yes
               appendfsync everysec
               maxmemory-policy noeviction
  One replica, asynchronous replication, automatic failover.

Statement: "With AOF fsync every second, the sorted set can safely be the source of truth for points, because at most about one second of increments can be lost."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. "At most one second" is already acknowledged data that disappears, and nobody can tell which increments were lost, so they cannot be replayed. A failover to the asynchronous replica can lose more than a second. And ZINCRBY is not idempotent: a retried worker job adds the points again, with no record to compare against. Points are facts derived from passed challenges and completed lessons, which live in PostgreSQL; the sorted set should be a projection of them that can be rebuilt (lesson 11) and checked by reconciliation. Redis Patterns covers this mistake in detail. The misconception is that persistence settings turn a cache into a system of record; durability is only one of the properties, the others are an audit trail, idempotent writes and the ability to rebuild.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_131') AND order_index = 1)
