    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_131'), 'Trace of POST /submissions in the new design (load test, 340 submissions/s):

  POST /submissions                         38 ms   202 {"submissionId":"sub_81f2","status":"queued"}
    validate + rate limit (Redis)             3 ms
    BEGIN                                     
      INSERT INTO submission (... ''queued'')   4 ms
      INSERT INTO outbox (submission.created) 2 ms
    COMMIT                                    9 ms
  (separate trace, linked)  grade sub_81f2   5.2 s   worker grader-7

Statement: "If grader-7 crashes after the API answered 202, the submission is not lost, because the submission row and its outbox row were committed before the API answered."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The API answers 202 only after COMMIT, so the submission and the event that triggers grading are both durable in PostgreSQL, the source of truth. If the worker dies mid-job, the queue redelivers the job after its visibility timeout (or a sweeper re-enqueues submissions still queued after N minutes), and grading runs again; the worker must therefore be idempotent (it writes the result with ON CONFLICT or a status check). This is the point of putting the sync/async boundary right after a durable write: the learner''s promise (R6a, accepted before the deadline) is kept even when everything after the boundary fails. The misconception is that asynchronous processing means a crash loses work; it only loses work when the hand-off is not durable.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_131') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
