-- challenge chal_coding_sdc_412 (mini_coding)
INSERT INTO challenge.challenges (public_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, name, title, description, status, difficulty, type, points, is_published, is_deleted)
VALUES ('chal_coding_sdc_412', (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'system-design-capstone'), (SELECT id FROM course.module WHERE course_id = (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'system-design-capstone') AND order_index = 4 AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), (SELECT id FROM course.lesson WHERE content_source = 'lesson-sdc-4-1' AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), 'vertical-slice-idempotent-end-to-end', 'Vertical slice: API to leaderboard, idempotent end to end', 'Build the CodeDev vertical slice for "complete a lesson": the API handler and progress service write the progress and an outbox event in one PostgreSQL transaction, a relay publishes the outbox to the event bus, and the leaderboard consumer adds the points to a Redis sorted set. Clients retry, the relay can die between publishing and marking a row, and the bus delivers at least once, so every hop must be idempotent: the same request retried returns the same response and changes nothing twice, a redelivered event counts once, and a crash at any step converges after the replay.

Write three functions (the fakes are in the file; use only the interfaces given):
1) completeLesson(req, ctx) -> ApiResponse. a) requestId, userId and lessonId must be non-empty strings, else { status: 400, error: "invalid_request" }. b) Everything else in ONE ctx.store.transaction, with the idempotency key "<userId>/<requestId>" (each client picks its request ids, so they are unique per learner only). If a request is saved under the key with the same lessonId, return its saved response unchanged; with another lessonId, return { status: 422, error: "request_id_reused" }. If lessonId is not an own key of ctx.lessonPoints (the server''s catalog: the client never sends points), return { status: 404, error: "unknown_lesson" } and save nothing. If the learner already completed the lesson, respond { status: 200, lessonId, newlyCompleted: false, totalPoints } and change nothing else. Otherwise append lessonId to progress.completed, add its points, append the outbox event { eventId: "lesson-completed:<userId>:<lessonId>", userId, lessonId, points } and respond { status: 200, lessonId, newlyCompleted: true, totalPoints } (totalPoints after the change). Save every 200 response with tx.putRequest(key, { lessonId, response }) in the same transaction. A learner without a progress row starts at { completed: [], points: 0 }. Let exceptions from the store propagate (a crash must stay a crash).
2) relayOutbox(ctx): for each row of ctx.store.unpublished() (seq order), ctx.bus.publish(row.event), then ctx.store.markPublished(row.seq).
3) onLessonCompleted(event, ctx): in one ctx.board.atomically call, return if board.isProcessed(event.eventId); otherwise markProcessed(event.eventId) and incrementBy(event.userId, event.points).

Input: { lessonPoints, script }. Script steps: { request, crash? } calls completeLesson; crash "beforeCommit" kills the process just before its first transaction commits (nothing is saved), "afterCommit" right after it commits (saved, but the client gets no response). { relay: true, crashAtPublish?: N, crashAfterPublish?: N } runs relayOutbox; the process dies at the Nth publish of that step, before or after the message reaches the bus. { deliver: true, crashBeforeAck?: N } delivers the bus queue in order; the process dies after handling the Nth message and before its ack, so that message stays at the head of the queue. After the script, a final replay runs relayOutbox and delivers the whole queue with no crash.
Output (JSON): responses (one per request step: the response, or "connection lost"), progress per learner (completed sorted, points), events (outbox event ids in seq order), board ([userId, points], highest first, ties by userId) and errors (unexpected exceptions).', 'open', 'hard'::difficulty_question, 'mini_coding', 40, true, false)
ON CONFLICT (public_id) DO UPDATE SET
    course_id = EXCLUDED.course_id, module_id = EXCLUDED.module_id, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, name = EXCLUDED.name,
    title = EXCLUDED.title, description = EXCLUDED.description, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, type = EXCLUDED.type,
    points = EXCLUDED.points, is_published = true, is_deleted = false, updated_at = now();
DELETE FROM challenge.challenge_hints WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_sdc_412');
INSERT INTO challenge.challenge_hints (challenge_id, level, body)
VALUES ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_sdc_412'), 1, 'For each crash point, ask what the next attempt finds. If the process dies right after the commit, which rows exist, and what must the retry with the same requestId find so that it can answer exactly what the first attempt would have answered?'),
       ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_sdc_412'), 2, 'Look at what goes into the single transaction of completeLesson: the progress, the outbox event and the saved response must all be written there, in one commit. In the consumer, the processed check, the mark and the increment belong inside one atomically call. In the relay, ask which order of publish and markPublished can lose an event and which can only duplicate it.'),
       ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_sdc_412'), 3, 'A similar slice for a course purchase: key "<userId>/<checkoutId>"; one transaction reads the saved checkout (same cart: return the saved answer; another cart: 422), otherwise inserts the order, an outbox row { eventId: "order-paid:ord_17" } and the saved answer. The receipt-email consumer keeps a set of sent event ids, and checks and adds to it in the same atomic step that queues the email.');
