  POST /lessons/:id/complete: AuthGuard, then progress service -> PostgreSQL (progress + outbox)
  GET /leaderboard: Redis sorted set (shows "unavailable" when Redis fails, lesson 9)
Redis: redis-main primary + 1 replica (Sentinel), both in subnet 10.20.8.0/24
Cause: at 14:02 a security group change dropped every packet from the API subnet to 10.20.8.0/24 (no reset, no error: connections just hang)

Evidence D - the review board''s questions:
1. POST /lessons/:id/complete does not read the catalog. Why did it fail?
2. Would a second Redis replica have prevented this?
3. Should we run 12 API instances instead of 6?
4. What is the fix, what does it cost PostgreSQL, and how will you prove it before exam day?

Statement: "Board question 2: yes. A second Redis replica with automatic failover would have kept the API up, so the read path can stay as it is."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The replica that existed did not help, and a second one would not either: B says the security group change dropped packets from the API subnet to the whole Redis subnet, so primary and replicas were unreachable together, and Sentinel had no reason to fail over because Redis itself was healthy. Even when a failover does happen, it takes seconds to tens of seconds, and with 30 s waits the 240 slots fill in under a second. Replicas protect against one kind of failure (a dead Redis node); they do not change the fact that the read path cannot answer without the cache. The misconception is that redundancy of a component removes it as a single point of failure; the dependency is the problem, not the number of nodes.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_431') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
