Proposed fix (PR #918, "make the cache optional"):
  async get(key: string) {
    try {
      const cached = await this.redis.get(key);     // client settings unchanged (C)
      if (cached !== null) return cached;
    } catch {
      // Redis failed: fall through to PostgreSQL
    }
    return this.postgres.load(key);
  }

Statement: "Board question 4: PR #918 is the fix. Once a Redis error falls back to PostgreSQL, the 30 s timeout no longer matters."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A dropped packet is not an error: redis.get only rejects when the 30 s connect timeout (or never, for commandTimeoutMs 0) expires, so every request would still hold a slot for 30 s before reaching the fallback, and the API would go down the same way. The fallback needs a short timeout of its own (50 ms, far above Redis''s healthy p99 of about 1.2 ms), a circuit breaker so that, once Redis is clearly down, requests stop paying even those 50 ms, and single-flight per key so that a hot key does not send hundreds of identical queries to PostgreSQL''s 20 connections. The misconception is that a try/catch makes a dependency optional: only a bound on how long you wait does.', 5, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_431') AND order_index = 5)
    RETURNING id
)
