Verification plan in the review doc: "Chaos test in staging at exam-day load (340 submissions/s plus about 250 API reads/s): stop the Redis process for 10 minutes. Pass if read success stays >= 99.9%, p95 <= 300 ms and PostgreSQL connections stay below the pool size."

Statement: "This test alone does not prove the fix for Thursday''s failure: a stopped Redis refuses connections within a millisecond, so the plan must also make Redis hang (drop its packets, as on Thursday) to exercise the 50 ms timeout."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Stopping the process gives the client a connection refused error at once, which a plain try/catch already handles; it never tests the timeout, which is exactly what failed on Thursday. The chaos test must cover both modes: refuse (stop the process) and hang (drop packets with a firewall rule or a fault-injection proxy), and also the recovery (Redis back empty: the breaker closes, misses refill the cache, PostgreSQL load falls back). The pass criteria are good: they are the user-facing SLO (p95 300 ms for reads) plus the resource that would fail next (the PostgreSQL pool). The misconception is that any chaos test proves resilience; it proves only the failure mode it injects.', 6, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_431') AND order_index = 6)
    RETURNING id
)
