// api/certificates/client.ts
export const certClient = createHttpClient({
  baseUrl: "http://cert-svc.internal",
  timeoutMs: 2_000,                         // per attempt
  retry: { retries: 3, backoffMs: 200, factor: 2 },   // waits 200, 400, 800 ms
});

// POST /lessons/:id/complete still awaits certClient.post("/certificates", ...) inline

The certificate service hangs again: it accepts connections and never answers.

Statement: "With this patch, a hung certificate service can hold a POST /lessons/:id/complete request for at most about 2 seconds."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The 2 s limit applies to each attempt, and the client makes up to 4 attempts (1 + 3 retries) with 200 + 400 + 800 ms of backoff in between: 4 x 2,000 + 1,400 = 9,400 ms. Every completion request still holds an API request slot for over 9 s, so with enough course completions the slots can still run out, just more slowly than with no timeout. Worse, all those retries go to a service that is already hung. The misconception is that a per-attempt timeout bounds the request; only a deadline per request does that, with each call''s timeout taken from what the request has left and retries stopped when it is spent. The real fix was to take the certificate off the request path: publish CourseCompleted and let a job issue it with its own timeout and retries.', 1, 5
