Code search for the provider client (payments/providerClient.ts):
  api/payments/checkout.ts          POST /checkout           (4 s timeout, 1 retry with idempotency key)
  api/payments/webhook.ts           POST /webhooks/payments  (inbound, writes course_access)
  jobs/reconcilePayments.ts         every 10 min

Entitlement check used by course, lesson, progress and submission routes:
  SELECT 1 FROM course_access WHERE user_id = $1 AND course_id = $2

API dashboard, 19:10-19:52:
  POST /checkout              5xx 96 %
  GET  /courses/:id           p95 125 ms   5xx 0.1 %
  POST /lessons/:id/progress  p95 98 ms    5xx 0.1 %
  POST /submissions           p95 410 ms   5xx 0.2 %

Statement: "The blast radius of this outage was limited to buying courses: learners who already had access kept learning, because no route on the learning path waits for the provider."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Only checkout, the inbound webhook and the reconciliation job use the provider client; every learning route checks access with a PostgreSQL query on course_access, which was written when each earlier payment was confirmed. The dashboard agrees: checkout failed while course pages, progress and submissions kept their normal latency and error rate. This is the containment you want for a dependency you cannot fix yourself. Note what the evidence does not cover: a learner charged just before the outage whose webhook was lost would wait until the 10-minute reconciliation job grants access, which is why that job exists. The misconception is that an external SPOF must be removed; often it is enough to keep it on one journey and make its unknown outcomes recoverable.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_511') AND order_index = 2)
