14:02:09.104  api       ZINCRBY leaderboard:global 30 u_5512 -> OK   (acknowledged by redis-lb-1)
14:02:09.300  redis-lb-1 master_repl_offset 88,412,930
14:02:09.300  redis-lb-2 slave_repl_offset  88,411,018   (1,912 bytes behind)
14:02:09.310  redis-lb-1 host lost (zone a)
14:02:14.870  sentinel  +switch-master redis-lb  redis-lb-1 -> redis-lb-2

The design doc says: "Leaderboard: Redis with a replica and automatic failover, so no single point of failure."

Statement: "Because the leaderboard has a replica with automatic failover, this failover cannot lose acknowledged score updates, so the leaderboard does not need a rebuild path from PostgreSQL."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Redis replicates asynchronously by default: the primary acknowledges a write before the replica has it. At 14:02:09.300 the replica was 1,912 bytes behind, and the ZINCRBY acknowledged at 14:02:09.104 may well be in those bytes; when the replica was promoted, any such write was lost although the API had been told OK. The replica removes the availability SPOF (the leaderboard is back in about 5 s), but it does not make the data durable. That is why CodeDev keeps PostgreSQL as the source of truth, treats the sorted set as a projection, and can rebuild it or reconcile counts after a failover (lesson 11). The misconception is that redundancy for availability is the same as protection against data loss; they are separate questions in a failure-mode table.', 3, 5
