    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_521'), 'GET /courses/:id/progress today, with the availability each dependency had over the last 12 months:

API instances (3, across zones)    99.99 %
Redis cache (single primary)       99.9 %    a cache error makes the request fail with 503
PostgreSQL (managed HA)            99.95 %

Proposal: "Move PostgreSQL to a multi-region setup with a published 99.99% availability, for about $1,500 more per month. Nothing else changes."

Statement: "After this upgrade, the progress read path can reach 99.99% availability, which cuts the downtime learners can see to about 4 minutes a month."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The three dependencies are in series: the request fails if any of them fails. After the upgrade the path is at most 0.9999 x 0.999 x 0.9999, about 99.88%, roughly 52 minutes of possible downtime a month, not 4. The Redis cache, which turns its own errors into 503s, now limits the path, so the $1,500 a month buys very little. The cheaper nine is to take Redis off the critical path: a short timeout and a fallback to PostgreSQL on a cache error (lessons 9 and 12), after which the path is 0.9999 x 0.9995, about 99.94%, with no new region. The misconception is that the availability of one component is the availability of the journey; compute the journey from its dependencies before buying any of them a better number.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdc_521') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
